---
import { getPublishedCollection } from 'astro-course-university/content';
import { withBase } from 'astro-theme-university/url';
import CourseLayout from '../layouts/CourseLayout.astro';
import ConnectionArt from '../components/ConnectionArt.astro';
import { courseMeta } from '../course-config';
import { formatCourseDate } from '../lib/dates';
const assessments = (await getPublishedCollection('assessments')).sort((a,b) => a.data.week-b.data.week);
const [titleMain, titleSpecial] = courseMeta.title.split(' & ');
---
<CourseLayout title={courseMeta.title} description={courseMeta.description} home>
  <div class="home-content">
    <section class="home-hero" aria-labelledby="course-title">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Department of Computational Courtship · Slop University</span>
        <h1 id="course-title" class="technical-title">{titleMain} <em>&amp; {titleSpecial}</em></h1>
        <p class="intro">Your love life has a loss function.</p>
        <p class="description">A fourth-year CS course in algorithmic romance: from profile photos and bio tests to first-date Markov chains and graceful endings. Every optimisation ships with its assumptions.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href={withBase('/lectures/week-01/')}>Start with week 1 <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/toolkit/')}>Open the lab toolkit <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note">Bring probability and pseudocode. Relationship status: not a dependency.</p>
      </div>
      <ConnectionArt />
    </section>

    <dl class="facts-strip">
      <div><dt>Your course</dt><dd>{courseMeta.code} · Level {courseMeta.level} undergraduate</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Teaching period</dt><dd>{courseMeta.session}, {courseMeta.year}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>The commitment</dt><dd>12 weeks · 6 hours a week</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Your final release</dt><dd>A profile. A benchmark. A defence.</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <div class="home-columns">
      <section aria-labelledby="about-course">
        <span class="eyebrow">For engineers who read the assumptions</span>
        <h2 id="about-course">What did you actually optimize?</h2>
        <p>Dating apps give you recommendations, not their source code. A better response rate might be a better bio. It might also be Tuesday. This course turns that ambiguity into twelve weeks of experiments, algorithms and model criticism.</p>
        <p>Work on Null Island: an invented local market with downloadable data and one fictional profile to release. Audit a toy ranking system, encounter a statistical reversal, trace a conversation through a Markov chain, and discover why a perfect rubric score can still produce a terrible invitation.</p>
        <h3>By the end, you can…</h3>
        <ol class="outcomes">
          <li>Define a sampling frame and an experiment that separates a profile change from an exposure change.</li>
          <li>Implement ranking, mutual-selection, messaging and conversation-state models under explicit assumptions.</li>
          <li>Evaluate a frozen candidate with uncertainty, sensitivity tests and an exact simulation check.</li>
          <li>Deploy a reproducible fictional profile with a threat model, maintenance plan and defensible top-1% claim.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>
      <aside class="notice" aria-labelledby="benchmark-title">
        <span class="eyebrow">Read the fine print. It is the course.</span>
        <h3 id="benchmark-title">Top 1% of 100 invented entries.</h3>
        <p>One candidate. Ninety-nine synthetic controls. A published quality score and a tie rule that does not flatter you.</p>
        <p>We grade your engineering, not your rank. No app account, real profile or successful date is required.</p>
        <a href={withBase('/toolkit/#benchmark')}>Inspect the benchmark <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">The semester, in three chapters</span><h2>Measure. Model. Ship.</h2></div>
      <a href={withBase('/weeks/')}>View all twelve dated weeks <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-grid">
      <section class="phase-card"><span class="eyebrow">01 / Weeks 1–3</span><h3>Measure.</h3><p>The course boundary, platform architecture and photo assets. Establish what a source, a denominator and an image can actually tell you.</p><a href={withBase('/sessions/01-system-boundary/')}>Draw the system boundary →</a></section>
      <section class="phase-card"><span class="eyebrow">02 / Weeks 4–7</span><h3>Model.</h3><p>NLP and bio A/B tests, mutual-selection probability, opening messages and asynchronous response games. Every protocol needs a stop state.</p><a href={withBase('/sessions/04-bio-experiment/')}>Write the bio experiment protocol →</a></section>
      <section class="phase-card"><span class="eyebrow">03 / Weeks 8–12</span><h3>Ship.</h3><p>Threat detection, offline handover, Markov first dates, follow-up and maintenance. Build the release and document how it can end.</p><a href={withBase('/sessions/08-threat-model/')}>Inspect the threat model →</a></section>
    </div>

    <div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">Assessment · 100% accounted for</span><h2>Evidence before confidence.</h2></div><a href={withBase('/assessments/')}>Read the briefs →</a></div>
    <div class="assessment-list">
      {assessments.map((assessment, index) => <a class="assessment-row" href={withBase(`/assessments/${assessment.id}/`)}>
        <span class="eyebrow">0{index + 1}</span><strong>{assessment.data.title}</strong><span class="due">Week {assessment.data.week} · {formatCourseDate(assessment.data.due)}</span><span class="weight">{assessment.data.weight}%</span>
      </a>)}
    </div>

    <div class="home-columns">
      <section><span class="eyebrow">Before you enrol</span><h2>Some prerequisites. No prerequisites for love.</h2><p>Expected background: introductory probability, basic statistical inference, data structures and programming in one language. You should be able to read pseudocode, manipulate a CSV and explain a simple algorithm's runtime. No machine-learning framework is required.</p><p>Mondays: lecture, 11 am–12 pm. Thursdays: lab, 2–3.30 pm. Both meet in Systems Lab 2. Allow another 3½ hours for reading, implementation and assessment. Teaching runs from {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.startDate)} to {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.endDate)}, with a break from 5–18 April.</p><a class="button secondary" href={withBase('/people/')}>Meet your teaching team →</a></section>
      <aside class="notice"><span class="eyebrow">Try a calculation</span><h3>Bio B got more replies.</h3><p>120/1,000 versus 140/1,000. Is that enough evidence to ship? Change the sample and inspect the assumptions.</p><a href={withBase('/toolkit/#experiment')}>Open the A/B sandbox ↗</a></aside>
    </div>
    <p class="bottom-note">A fictional Slop University course. Real methods, synthetic observations, and no warranty on human behaviour. Hinge and Tinder are case studies, not course partners.</p>
  </div>
</CourseLayout>
